#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""scopes — the groups of repos the owner works on together, and the VS Code workspace of each.

  scopes                  each scope, its file and its repos, the anchor first
  scopes of REPO          the scope REPO is in (a registry key, or any path inside its checkout
                          or one of its worktrees); exit 1 when it is in none
  scopes anchor REPO      the absolute path of the first folder of REPO's scope's file — read from
                          the file when it exists, else the first declared repo on this Mac
  scopes workspace REPO   the absolute path of REPO's scope's workspace file, when it exists;
                          exit 1 otherwise
  scopes write [--dry-run] [--from PATH]
                          write every scope's workspace file where repos.json says it lives,
                          keeping the worktree folders VS Code added; move a retired file into
                          ~/projects/.scopes-retired/ (one tracked in git is named, never moved).
                          A file a VS Code window has open is never moved, nor rewritten with a
                          new first folder: refused with a ✗ line and exit 1 (2026-10-15 crash).
                          A file inside a git checkout goes into its .git/info/exclude. --from:
                          a session at PATH (wharf's sync.py at every start) — every open scope,
                          a confidential one only when PATH is under one of its own folders, no
                          retired file moved; it waits up to LOCK_WAIT for another write's lock
                          and exits 3, writing nothing, if it never frees; a fault naming a
                          confidential scope PATH is outside reads as one line naming none of it
  scopes check            exit 1, naming each fault, when a repo is in no scope or two, a scope
                          names a repo the registry lacks, two scopes share a file, an anchor
                          is a glob or a twin, a flag is not true/false, a login no label or a
                          yard no host, a confidential scope's anchor is on this Mac without a
                          `memory/` folder, or a confidential scope's yard is a host its anchor
                          is not on (the check runs this through bin/test_scopes.sh)
  scopes set SCOPE [--confidential | --open] [--login LABEL | --no-login] [--yard HOST|none] [--dry-run]
                          flag SCOPE confidential (a node nothing outside it reads) or open, name
                          the account label its runs start on, and the host its ships are driven
                          from (its yard: a host lighthouse/hosts lists, a confidential scope's
                          own node — the core's scope_yard_hosts — or none, written as
                          null); each flag optional, at least one given; writes repos.json in
                          place, its layout kept, and proves the file then parses to exactly that
                          change
  scopes configure        the checklist: every scope, its flag and login; at a terminal, toggle
                          by number and save through `set`
  scopes paths SCOPE      the paths the fence keeps out for SCOPE (the core's scope_paths)

Confidential (knowledge-layers design D3, 2026-10-20: "base it on scopes. And make a scope
configurator on regatta that lets me set which ones are confidential"): `"confidential": true` on a
scope is the registry of what is fenced, `"login"` the label `account pick --repo` hands its runs
to, `"yard"` the host whose tick drives its ships and where `wharf apply --yard` lays them
(skipper-resilience S2; `null` or absent, no yard: a plain apply as before). The core reads them
(`common.confidential_scopes()`, `scope_login()`, `scope_yard()`, `scope_paths()`); this tool only
writes them and checks them. A flagged scope's anchor is its knowledge repo, which holds
`memory/<repo>/` for the scope's repos (D1), so `check` fails one that is here without it.

The list is `scopes` in repos.json (the owner, 2026-10-14: "create reasonable groups of projects that
I might work on together. Connect [the layer] in each of them ... Let's call this 'scope'"); the
reasoning sits beside it as `_scopes`, the answers in docs/scopes-2026-10-14.md.

One file per scope, where the owner keeps it (D1-B, D2-A): the list is what is canonical, the files are
VS Code's to write into — each time a session enters a worktree, VS Code adds it as a folder, so a
file in git would dirty its repo at every entry. `write` owns the folders it lists and the
settings repos.json declares (D4); it keeps any folder under `.claude/worktrees/` that still exists
and every other top-level key, and drops a setting nobody declared — except OWNED_ELSEWHERE.
`check` checks the list only.

Two other tools write a scope file, and `write` keeps the peace with both (review 2026-10-15):
The home repo's scripts/sync_vscode_workspace.py rebuilds its own .code-workspace's folders on every commit
(the root, then its worktrees, then whatever else is there), and hooks/sync.py keeps
`git.branchProtection` in it. Both parse it as strict JSON — a `//` header made the home repo's script
rebuild the file from nothing — so `write` writes plain JSON, puts the anchor's worktree folders
straight after the anchor (the order the home repo's script makes), leaves `git.branchProtection` as it
finds it, and compares content, not text, so neither tool's formatting starts a rewrite. It writes
in place, never by rename: a rename over a watched workspace file is the deleted-path event that
crashed a window on 2026-10-15, and an in-place rewrite crashed none.

The anchor comes first: the Claude extension scopes a window's session list, and the cwd of every
session it starts, to the window's FIRST folder (`sessionListScopeRoot` = `workspaceFolders[0]`,
extension 2.1.299). A session for another repo of the scope still opens in the scope's window and
moves into that repo's worktree from there (the owner, D2: "I want the session to open in the scoped
window even if it's not targeting the first folder"); bin/pickup finds that window by any of its
folders, and a session picked up again by the folder whose project dir holds the transcript. Then the scope's other
repos in registry order, twins last, then the layer (likely to change whatever the work is).

A member may be a glob (`*zigzag*`), matched against registry keys, so a repo not cloned yet
lands in its scope the day it is added. Paths in a file are relative to it, so one list serves
both Macs; a folder missing on this Mac is left out of the file, with a note.

Python 3.9, stdlib only.
"""
import datetime
import fcntl
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import time
import urllib.parse
from pathlib import Path

sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / "lib"))
import core_path  # noqa: E402 — the regatta core (C2); none: one line, exit 2
core_path.require()
import common  # noqa: E402

# The registry through the core's one reader (C3); the checkout it really sits in (an overlay links
# it into the config dir) is the one whose primary's parent holds every repo and scope file.
REGISTRY = common.repos_json()
HERE = REGISTRY.resolve().parent if REGISTRY else None
# The registry's own repo (the knowledge layer, on the owner's Macs), by its registry key: every
# scope's file lists it last. Read from where the file really sits, never named (as github-repo).
LAYER = HERE.name if HERE else None
WORKTREES = "/.claude/worktrees/"
RETIRED = ".scopes-retired"
EXT = ".code-workspace"
def _lock_wait():
    """Seconds a session start's `write --from` waits for another write's lock: REGATTA_SCOPES_LOCK_WAIT,
    else 60, kept within 0-100 so sync.py's 120 s timeout never cuts a wait short; a value that does not
    read is 60, never a crash of every verb at import (the second finish review, 2026-11-03)."""
    try:
        v = float(os.environ.get("REGATTA_SCOPES_LOCK_WAIT") or 60)
    except ValueError:
        v = 60.0
    return 60.0 if v != v else min(max(v, 0.0), 100.0)


LOCK_WAIT = _lock_wait()
# this tool by its own path: a start note reaches every repo's session, where `bin/scopes` resolves nowhere
# (the second finish review, 2026-11-03)
SELF = str(Path(__file__).resolve())
OWNED_ELSEWHERE = ("git.branchProtection",)  # hooks/sync.py's landed-branch locks: never declared, never dropped


def registry_data(root=None):
    """The registry's one parse: every decision a run makes reads this dict, never the file again."""
    if not root and not REGISTRY:
        sys.exit(f"scopes: no registry — set CLAUDE_REPOS_JSON, or link one at {common.CONFIG / 'wharf' / 'repos.json'}")
    p = root / "repos.json" if root else REGISTRY
    try:
        return json.loads(p.read_text())
    except OSError as e:   # a dangling link: the core returns it, so the read refuses here
        sys.exit(f"scopes: the registry {p} cannot be read — {e.strerror}")


def registry(root=None, data=None):
    data = data if data is not None else registry_data(root)
    return data["repos"], data.get("scopes") or {}, data.get("folder_names") or {}


def inside(name, src, reg):
    """Is path `src` under one of scope `name`'s own folders — the core's scope_paths: its checkouts,
    their worktrees, its transcripts and login dirs, from `reg`, the run's one parse? Never by a slug
    in the path, which scope_match also counts: a scratch folder named after the scope is not in it
    (AGENTS.md's guard-scratch trap; the /finish review, 2026-11-01)."""
    s = os.path.realpath(src)
    return any(s == r or s.startswith(r + os.sep)
               for r in (os.path.realpath(p) for p in common.scope_paths(name, reg=reg)))


def primary(root=None):
    """The primary checkout of the registry's repo (the git common dir's parent)."""
    root = root or HERE
    r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(root), "rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-common-dir"],
                       capture_output=True, text=True)
    return Path(r.stdout.strip()).parent if r.returncode == 0 and r.stdout.strip() else root


def projects(root=None):
    """The folder every registry key and scope file is relative to: the primary checkout's parent;
    a registry outside any checkout (a plain file in the config dir), the core's `projects` key."""
    p = primary(root)
    return p.parent if (p / ".git").exists() else common.PROJECTS


is_glob = common.is_glob
members = common.scope_members   # the scope's repos, in its order, globs matched (the core's one rule)


def faults(repos, scopes):
    out, seen, files = [], {}, {}
    for name, scope in scopes.items():
        first = (scope.get("repos") or [None])[0]
        if not first or is_glob(first):
            out.append(f"scope {name}'s first entry must name its anchor outright, not {first!r}")
        for k in OWNED_ELSEWHERE:
            if k in (scope.get("settings") or {}):
                out.append(f"scope {name} declares {k}, which hooks/sync.py owns")
        if not scope.get("file"):
            out.append(f"scope {name} has no file")
        elif scope["file"] in files:
            out.append(f"scopes {files[scope['file']]} and {name} would both write {scope['file']}")
        else:
            files[scope["file"]] = name
        for key in members(scope, repos):
            if key not in repos:
                out.append(f"scope {name} names {key}, which repos.json does not list")
            if key in seen:
                out.append(f"{key} is in two scopes: {seen[key]} and {name}")
            seen.setdefault(key, name)
        if first and not is_glob(first) and repos.get(first, {}).get("twin_of"):
            out.append(f"scope {name}'s anchor {first} is a twin; a twin is never worked in")
        if "confidential" in scope and not isinstance(scope["confidential"], bool):
            out.append(f"scope {name}'s confidential is {scope['confidential']!r}, not true or false")
        if "login" in scope and not (isinstance(scope["login"], str) and scope["login"]):
            out.append(f"scope {name}'s login is {scope['login']!r}, not an account label")
        yard = scope.get("yard")
        if "yard" in scope and yard is not None and not (isinstance(yard, str) and yard):
            out.append(f"scope {name}'s yard is {yard!r}, not a host or null")
        elif yard and scope.get("confidential") is True and first and not is_glob(first):
            # the yard keeps the ship's plan, its pinned blueprint and its workers' worktrees: a
            # confidential scope's yard is a host its anchor (the knowledge repo) is on, so the
            # node's text stays on the Macs the registry already says hold it
            hosts = (repos.get(first) or {}).get("hosts")
            if not (isinstance(hosts, list) and yard in hosts):
                out.append(f"confidential scope {name}'s yard {yard} is not a host its anchor {first} is on"
                           f" ({first}'s hosts: {', '.join(hosts) if isinstance(hosts, list) and hosts else 'none listed'})")
    for key in repos:
        if key not in seen:
            out.append(f"{key} is in no scope — add it to \"scopes\" in repos.json")
    return out


def confidential_faults(repos, scopes, base):
    """(faults, notes) for the flagged scopes: an anchor here with no memory/ is a fault (D1: the
    node's memory lives in its knowledge repo); an anchor not cloned on this Mac is a note."""
    out, notes = [], []
    for name, scope in scopes.items():
        if scope.get("confidential") is not True:
            continue
        first = (scope.get("repos") or [None])[0]
        if not first or is_glob(first):
            continue   # faults() names it
        if not (base / first).is_dir():
            notes.append(f"confidential scope {name}'s anchor {first} is not on this Mac — its memory/ unchecked here")
        elif not (base / first / "memory").is_dir():
            out.append(f"confidential scope {name}'s anchor {first} has no memory/ — its repos' memory has no home"
                       f" in the node (make {base / first / 'memory'}, or atlas's knowledge-home init {name})")
    return out, notes


# repos.json is laid out by hand (one-line rows, aligned lists), so `set` edits the text of one
# scope's object and never re-dumps the file: a dump would rewrite every line of it. `spans` finds
# where each value sits; the edit is then proved structurally — the file must parse to exactly
# the old registry with the one change, or nothing is written.
_WS = " \t\r\n"


def spans(text, i=0):
    """→ (start, end, children) for the JSON value at text[i:]; children maps each key of an
    object to (key_start, value_start, value_end, its children). Only objects get children."""
    while text[i] in _WS:
        i += 1
    start, c = i, text[i]
    if c == "{":
        kids, i = {}, i + 1
        while True:
            while text[i] in _WS + ",":
                i += 1
            if text[i] == "}":
                return start, i + 1, kids
            k0 = i
            k, i = json.JSONDecoder().raw_decode(text, i)
            while text[i] in _WS + ":":
                i += 1
            v0, v1, sub = spans(text, i)
            kids[k] = (k0, v0, v1, sub)
            i = v1
    if c == "[":
        i += 1
        while True:
            while text[i] in _WS + ",":
                i += 1
            if text[i] == "]":
                return start, i + 1, {}
            i = spans(text, i)[1]
    _, end = json.JSONDecoder().raw_decode(text, i)
    return start, end, {}


class _Null:
    """set_key's value for a JSON `null` written out (`--yard none`): None already means the pair
    removed, and a scope that says `"yard": null` has decided it has none."""

    def __repr__(self):
        return "NULL"


NULL = _Null()


def set_key(text, name, key, value):
    """repos.json's text with scopes.<name>.<key> set to `value` (None removes it, NULL writes
    `null`), the rest of the file byte for byte. Raises ValueError when the edit would not parse to
    exactly that."""
    want = json.loads(text)
    if name not in (want.get("scopes") or {}):
        raise ValueError(f"no scope {name} in the registry")
    if value is None:
        want["scopes"][name].pop(key, None)
    else:
        want["scopes"][name][key] = None if value is NULL else value
    dumped = json.dumps(None if value is NULL else value)
    o0, o1, kids = spans(text)[2]["scopes"][3][name][1:]
    if key in kids:
        k0, v0, v1, _ = kids[key]
        if value is not None:
            out = text[:v0] + dumped + text[v1:]
        else:   # the pair and the comma that joined it to its neighbour; a line of its own goes whole
            line = text.rfind("\n", 0, k0) + 1
            own = re.match(r"[ \t]*,[ \t]*\n", text[v1:]) if not text[line:k0].strip() else None
            after = re.match(r"\s*,[ \t]*", text[v1:])
            if own:
                out = text[:line] + text[v1 + own.end():]
            elif after:
                out = text[:k0] + text[v1 + after.end():]
            else:
                before = re.search(r",\s*$", text[o0:k0])
                out = text[:o0 + before.start()] + text[v1:] if before else text[:k0] + text[v1:]
    elif value is None:
        out = text
    else:
        body = text[o0 + 1:o1 - 1]
        pair = f'"{key}": {dumped}'
        m = re.match(r"\n([ \t]*)", body)
        if m:      # a multi-line object: a line of its own at the first key's indent
            out = text[:o0 + 1] + f"\n{m.group(1)}{pair}," + text[o0 + 1:]
        elif body.strip():   # the closing brace's own line and indent stay where they were
            head = text[:o1 - 1]
            kept = head.rstrip()
            out = kept + f", {pair}" + head[len(kept):] + text[o1 - 1:]
        else:
            out = text[:o0 + 1] + pair + text[o1 - 1:]
    if json.loads(out) != want:
        raise ValueError(f"the edit of scope {name}'s {key} did not parse back to the change alone — nothing written")
    return out


def flag_line(name, scope):
    on = scope.get("confidential") is True
    login = scope.get("login")
    yard = (f" — yard {scope['yard'] or 'none'}" if "yard" in scope else "")   # said only where the key is
    return f"{'[x]' if on else '[ ]'} {name}" + (f" — login {login}" if login else "") + yard


def set_flags(path, name, confidential=None, login=None, drop_login=False, dry=False, yard=None):
    """Write scope `name`'s flags into the registry file at `path`. → the lines to print. `yard`:
    a host, `none` (written as null), or None to leave the key as it is."""
    text = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    new = text
    if confidential is not None:
        new = set_key(new, name, "confidential", True if confidential else None)
    if login:
        new = set_key(new, name, "login", login)
    elif drop_login:
        new = set_key(new, name, "login", None)
    if yard is not None:
        new = set_key(new, name, "yard", NULL if yard == "none" else yard)
    scope = json.loads(new)["scopes"][name]
    if new == text:
        return [f"  {flag_line(name, scope)} — unchanged"]
    if not dry:
        write_registry(path, new)
    return [f"{'would write' if dry else 'wrote'} {path}: {flag_line(name, scope)}"]


def write_registry(path, text):
    """Replace the registry file in one step: every hook and the fence read it while this runs, and a
    truncate-then-write read half-way is no registry at all (the /finish review of S1, 2026-10-22)."""
    tmp = path.with_name(f".{path.name}.{os.getpid()}.tmp")
    tmp.write_text(text, encoding="utf-8")
    os.replace(tmp, path)


def primary_main(path):
    """Why `path` must not be written here: it sits in a repo's primary checkout on its main branch
    (the Macs' registry links to the overlay's primary, and a write there is live before any branch
    or pull request, and leaves main dirty for sync). None for a linked worktree, any other branch
    or no git repo."""
    def git(*a):
        r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(path.parent), *a], capture_output=True, text=True)
        return r.stdout.strip() if r.returncode == 0 else None
    branch, gd, common_dir = git("rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD"), git("rev-parse", "--absolute-git-dir"), \
        git("rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-common-dir")
    if branch in ("main", "master") and gd and common_dir and os.path.realpath(gd) == os.path.realpath(common_dir):
        return f"{path} is in the primary checkout of {Path(common_dir).parent}, on {branch}"
    return None


def configure(path, ask=input, dry=False, show=print):
    """The checklist: each scope with its flag; at a terminal, numbers toggle it and a label after
    one sets its login (`3 claude-x`); a blank line saves. → the lines to print."""
    data = json.loads(path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    names = list(data.get("scopes") or {})
    state = {n: (data["scopes"][n].get("confidential") is True, data["scopes"][n].get("login")) for n in names}
    first = dict(state)
    while True:
        for i, n in enumerate(names, 1):
            on, login = state[n]
            show(f"  {i:2} {'[x]' if on else '[ ]'} {n}" + (f" — login {login}" if login else ""))
        try:
            line = ask("toggle (numbers, `N LABEL` for a login, blank to save, q to quit): ").strip()
        except EOFError:
            line = "q"
        if line in ("q", "quit"):
            return ["nothing written"]
        if not line:
            break
        parts = line.replace(",", " ").split()
        n = 0
        while n < len(parts):
            if not parts[n].isdigit() or not 1 <= int(parts[n]) <= len(names):
                show(f"  ? {parts[n]} is no number on the list")
                n += 1
                continue
            key = names[int(parts[n]) - 1]
            on, login = state[key]
            if n + 1 < len(parts) and not parts[n + 1].isdigit():
                state[key] = (on, parts[n + 1])
                n += 2
            else:
                state[key] = (not on, login)
                n += 1
    out = []
    for n in names:
        if state[n] != first[n]:
            on, login = state[n]
            out += set_flags(path, n, on, login=login if login != first[n][1] else None, dry=dry)
    return out or ["nothing changed"]


def folders(scope, repos, layer=None):
    """The keys in the scope's file: the anchor (its first entry, named outright), the rest in
    order, twins last, then the layer (LAYER, the registry's own repo)."""
    layer = layer or LAYER
    keys = [k for k in members(scope, repos) if k in repos]
    first = (scope.get("repos") or [None])[0]
    if first in keys:
        keys.remove(first)
        keys.insert(0, first)
    if not keys:
        return [layer] if layer else []
    anchor, rest = keys[0], keys[1:]
    order = [anchor] + [k for k in rest if not repos[k].get("twin_of")] + [k for k in rest if repos[k].get("twin_of")]
    return order + ([] if not layer or layer in order else [layer])


def load_jsonc(text):
    """A workspace file VS Code wrote: JSON with // and /* */ comments and trailing commas."""
    out, i, n = [], 0, len(text)
    while i < n:
        c = text[i]
        if c == '"':
            j = i + 1
            while j < n and text[j] != '"':
                j += 2 if text[j] == "\\" else 1
            out.append(text[i:j + 1])
            i = j + 1
        elif text.startswith("//", i):
            i = text.find("\n", i) if "\n" in text[i:] else n
        elif text.startswith("/*", i):
            i = text.find("*/", i + 2) + 2 if "*/" in text[i + 2:] else n
        else:
            out.append(c)
            i += 1
    return json.loads(re.sub(r",(\s*[}\]])", r"\1", "".join(out)))


def render(name, scope, repos, names, base, current=None):
    """(body, notes): the file `write` makes for the scope at base/<file>, from the file there now."""
    path = base / scope["file"]
    notes = []
    managed = []
    for k in folders(scope, repos):
        if not (base / k).is_dir():
            notes.append(f"{k} is not on this Mac — left out of {scope['file']}")
            continue
        # a twin by its full key: `oss/shared` beside `shared`, never two of one name
        default = k if repos.get(k, {}).get("twin_of") else k.rsplit("/", 1)[-1]
        managed.append({"path": os.path.relpath(base / k, path.parent), "name": names.get(k, default)})
    body = dict(current or {})
    kept = []
    for f in (current or {}).get("folders") or []:
        p = str(f.get("path") or "")
        full = (path.parent / p) if not os.path.isabs(p) else Path(p)
        # VS Code writes them relative, `.claude/worktrees/<topic>` from an anchor's own folder
        if WORKTREES in "/" + p.replace("\\", "/") + "/" and full.is_dir():
            kept.append(f)
    body["folders"] = managed[:1] + kept + managed[1:]  # the home repo's script's order: root, worktrees, rest
    settings = dict(scope.get("settings") or {})
    for k in OWNED_ELSEWHERE:
        if k in ((current or {}).get("settings") or {}):
            settings[k] = current["settings"][k]
    body["settings"] = settings
    order = ["folders", "settings"] + [k for k in body if k not in ("folders", "settings")]
    return {k: body[k] for k in order}, notes


def anchor_of(scope, repos, base=None):
    """The scope's file's first folder — the window's anchor — read from the file when it is
    there (a refused write leaves an open file's old one), else the first declared repo present
    here, as render() would write it."""
    base = base or projects()
    path = base / scope["file"]
    try:
        f = first_folder(load_jsonc(path.read_text()), path)
        if f:
            return Path(f)
    except (OSError, ValueError, AttributeError, TypeError):
        pass
    keys = folders(scope, repos)
    return base / next((k for k in keys if (base / k).is_dir()), keys[0])


def resolve(arg, repos, base=None):
    """A registry key for ARG: the key itself, or the repo whose checkout (or worktree) holds the path."""
    if arg in repos:
        return arg
    p = Path(arg).expanduser()
    if not p.exists():
        return None
    r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(p if p.is_dir() else p.parent), "rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute",
                        "--git-common-dir"], capture_output=True, text=True)
    if r.returncode or not r.stdout.strip():
        return None
    top = Path(r.stdout.strip()).parent.resolve()
    try:
        key = str(top.relative_to((base or projects()).resolve()))
    except ValueError:
        return None
    return key if key in repos else None


def scope_of(key, repos, scopes):
    return next((n for n, s in scopes.items() if key in members(s, repos)), None) if key else None


# A file a VS Code window has open is never moved, and never rewritten with a new first folder.
# 2026-10-15 00:00: `write` moved the open regatta.code-workspace into .scopes-retired; VS Code
# logged "Watcher shutdown because watched path got deleted", its extension host ended two seconds
# later and nine Claude sessions died with it. The files rewritten in place, first folder unchanged,
# crashed nothing (the crash investigation, 2026-10-15).
# Which files are open: VS Code's own windowsState (storage.json, by path) or a window title in
# `code --status` ("… — atlas (Workspace) — …", by name) — either one is enough. VS Code running
# without `code --status` is "open" for every file: a refusal costs a rerun, a wrong guess a crash.
STORAGE = [Path.home() / "Library/Application Support/Code/User/globalStorage/storage.json",
           Path.home() / ".config/Code/User/globalStorage/storage.json"]
APP_CLI = Path("/Applications/Visual Studio Code.app/Contents/Resources/app/bin/code")


class Opened:
    """Which .code-workspace files VS Code windows have open, from the two readings."""

    def __init__(self, status=None, storage=None, running=False):
        self.paths, self.names = set(), set()
        for w in ([storage.get("windowsState", {}).get("lastActiveWindow")]
                  + (storage.get("windowsState", {}).get("openedWindows") or []) if storage else []):
            uri = ((w or {}).get("workspaceIdentifier") or {}).get("configURIPath") or ""
            if uri.startswith("file://"):
                self.paths.add(os.path.realpath(urllib.parse.unquote(uri[len("file://"):])))
        for m in re.finditer(r"window \[\d+\] \((.*)\)\s*$", status or "", re.M):
            self.names.update(re.findall(r"(?:^|— )([^—]+?) \(Workspace\)", m.group(1)))
        # storage.json is a snapshot (windowsState is saved on window blur and at shutdown, VS Code
        # 1.142 mainImpl.js): a window opened since is in `code --status` only, so without that
        # reading a running VS Code is blind, whatever storage.json says (review 2026-10-15)
        self.blind = running and status is None

    def holds(self, path):
        """Why `path` counts as open, or None."""
        if self.blind:
            return "VS Code is running and which files its windows have open could not be read"
        if os.path.realpath(path) in self.paths:
            return "a VS Code window has it open (storage.json)"
        stem = Path(path).name[:-len(EXT)] if str(path).endswith(EXT) else None
        if stem and stem in self.names:
            return f"a VS Code window titled \"{stem} (Workspace)\" is open"
        return None


def opened_now():
    """The live reading: `code --status` and storage.json, whichever can be read."""
    running = subprocess.run(["pgrep", "-f", "Visual Studio Code.app/Contents/MacOS"],
                             capture_output=True, text=True).returncode == 0
    cli = shutil.which("code") or (str(APP_CLI) if APP_CLI.exists() else None)
    status = None
    if cli and running:
        try:
            r = subprocess.run([cli, "--status"], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30)
            status = r.stdout if r.returncode == 0 and "window [" in r.stdout else None
        except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
            status = None
    storage = None
    for f in STORAGE:
        try:
            storage = json.loads(f.read_text())
            break
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            continue
    return Opened(status, storage, running)


def first_folder(body, path):
    f = ((body or {}).get("folders") or [{}])[0].get("path")
    return os.path.realpath(path.parent / f) if f else None


def tracked(path):
    r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(path.parent), "ls-files", "--error-unmatch", path.name],
                       capture_output=True, text=True)
    return r.returncode == 0


def exclude(path, dry):
    """Put scope file `path` in its checkout's .git/info/exclude when git would see it → the line
    to print, or None. The file is VS Code's to write into (it adds a folder at every worktree
    entry), so in git it would dirty the repo each time; the exclude is local, so a scope whose
    anchor is a fresh knowledge repo (2026-10-31) needs no commit there first. A line
    already there is never added again: a `.gitignore` that un-ignores the file beats it, and that
    is named instead (the /finish review, 2026-11-01: three runs had left three copies)."""
    def git(*a):
        return subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(path.parent), *a], capture_output=True, text=True)
    top = git("rev-parse", "--show-toplevel")
    if top.returncode != 0 or git("check-ignore", "-q", path.name).returncode == 0 or tracked(path):
        return None  # outside git, ignored already, or tracked (a retired-file case, named there)
    info = Path(git("rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-path", "info/exclude").stdout.strip())
    rel = "/" + os.path.relpath(os.path.realpath(path), os.path.realpath(top.stdout.strip()))
    # a pattern, not a path: escape what gitignore reads as one (the leading / already makes a
    # leading `#` or `!` literal); a trailing space would be dropped (the /finish review, 2026-11-03)
    rel = re.sub(r"([\\*?\[])", r"\\\1", rel)
    rel = rel[:-1] + "\\ " if rel.endswith(" ") else rel
    have = info.read_text() if info.exists() else ""
    if rel in (l.strip() for l in have.splitlines()):
        return f"⚠ {rel} is in {top.stdout.strip()}'s .git/info/exclude, but its .gitignore un-ignores it — git sees it"
    if not dry:
        info.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        info.write_text(have + ("" if not have or have.endswith("\n") else "\n") + rel + "\n")
    return f"{'would exclude' if dry else 'excluded'} {rel} in {top.stdout.strip()}'s .git/info/exclude"


def write(repos, scopes, names, base, dry, retired=(), opened=None, only=None):
    """Write every scope's file (only: the names to write, None for all); move each retired one
    aside. → the lines to print. A file a VS Code window has open (opened, read live when not
    given) is never moved, nor rewritten with another first folder: refused with a ✗ line, the
    rest done. VS Code is asked only once a file would change or move: every session start runs
    this, and `code --status` can take 30 s (the /finish review, 2026-11-01)."""
    seen = [opened]

    def held(p):
        if seen[0] is None:
            seen[0] = opened_now()
        return seen[0].holds(p)
    out = []
    for name, scope in scopes.items():
        if only is not None and name not in only:
            continue
        path = base / scope["file"]
        current, raw = None, None
        if path.exists():
            try:
                raw = path.read_text()
                current = load_jsonc(raw)
            except ValueError as e:
                out.append(f"✗ {scope['file']} is not readable JSON ({e}) — left as it is")
                continue
        if not path.parent.is_dir():
            out.append(f"  {scope['file']}: {path.parent} is not on this Mac — no file written")
            continue
        body, notes = render(name, scope, repos, names, base, current)
        out += [f"  {n}" for n in notes]
        kept_out = exclude(path, dry)   # before the write: git never sees the file appear
        if kept_out:
            out.append(kept_out)
        if current == body:
            continue  # content, not text: the home repo's script and sync.py format it their own way
        text = json.dumps(body, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False) + "\n"
        why = held(path) if current is not None else None
        if why and first_folder(current, path) != first_folder(body, path):
            out.append(f"✗ {scope['file']} not written: its first folder would change and {why} — close"
                       f" that window, then run `{SELF} write` (a session start writes it too: any"
                       f" start for an open scope, one inside it for a confidential one)")
            continue
        if (path.read_text() if path.exists() else None) != raw:
            # VS Code or sync.py's indicator wrote it while `code --status` ran: never over their
            # write with a body built from the old text (the /finish review, 2026-11-03)
            out.append(f"  {scope['file']} changed while this run read it — left for the next run")
            continue
        out.append(f"{'would write' if dry else 'wrote'} {scope['file']}")
        if not dry:
            path.write_text(text)   # in place, never a rename: the 2026-10-15 crash shape (header)
    stamp = datetime.date.today().isoformat()
    for rel in retired:
        path = base / rel
        if not path.exists():
            continue
        if tracked(path):
            out.append(f"⚠ {rel} is retired but tracked in git — remove it there with a commit")
            continue
        why = held(path)
        if why:
            out.append(f"✗ {rel} not retired: {why} — moving it would crash that window; close it,"
                       f" then run `{SELF} write` again")
            continue
        dest = base / RETIRED / stamp / rel.replace("/", "__")
        out.append(f"{'would move' if dry else 'moved'} retired {rel} → {dest.relative_to(base)}")
        if not dry:
            dest.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
            shutil.move(str(path), str(dest))
    return out


def main(argv):
    cmd = argv[0] if argv else "list"
    data = registry_data()
    repos, scopes, names = registry(data=data)
    if cmd in ("list", "-l"):
        for name, scope in scopes.items():
            flag = (" [confidential" + (f", login {scope['login']}" if scope.get("login") else "") + "]"
                    if scope.get("confidential") is True else "")
            print(f"{name}{flag} ({scope.get('file')}): " + ", ".join(members(scope, repos)))
        return 0
    if cmd == "set" and len(argv) >= 3:
        rest = argv[2:]
        want = True if "--confidential" in rest else False if "--open" in rest else None
        login = rest[rest.index("--login") + 1] if "--login" in rest and rest.index("--login") + 1 < len(rest) else None
        yard = rest[rest.index("--yard") + 1] if "--yard" in rest and rest.index("--yard") + 1 < len(rest) else None
        known = {"--confidential", "--open", "--login", "--no-login", "--yard", "--dry-run"}
        stray = [a for i, a in enumerate(rest) if a not in known and not (i and rest[i - 1] in ("--login", "--yard"))]
        if ("--confidential" in rest and "--open" in rest) or ("--login" in rest and (not login or login.startswith("-"))) \
                or ("--yard" in rest and (not yard or yard.startswith("-"))) \
                or stray or not known.intersection(rest) - {"--dry-run"}:
            print("scopes set: --confidential or --open (not both), --login takes a label, --yard a host or none"
                  + (f" — not understood: {' '.join(stray)}" if stray else ""), file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        if argv[1] not in scopes:
            print(f"scopes set: no scope {argv[1]} — the registry has {', '.join(scopes)}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        if yard and yard != "none":
            # a yard is a Mac the fleet knows (lighthouse's list, read through the core), or a confidential
            # scope's own node (the core's scope_yard_hosts: its node folder here, or this host being that node),
            # judged with the flag this write leaves and from one read of the registry it writes
            reg, torn = common.read_registry(REGISTRY.resolve())
            sc = ((reg or {}).get("scopes") or {}).get(argv[1])
            secret = want if want is not None else isinstance(sc, dict) and sc.get("confidential") is True
            if torn:
                hosts, nodes = common.config_lines("lighthouse/hosts") or [], []
                why = f"the registry {REGISTRY} does not read, so no node of {argv[1]}'s can be told"
            else:
                hosts, nodes, why = common.scope_yard_hosts(argv[1], reg=reg or {}, confidential=want)
            if yard not in hosts and yard.lower() not in nodes:   # a node's peer is matched case-blind
                if nodes:
                    node = f", nor {argv[1]}'s own node ({', '.join(nodes)})"
                elif why:
                    node = f", nor a node of {argv[1]}'s told here: {why}"
                elif secret:
                    node = (f", nor a node of {argv[1]}'s told here (a folder {common.CONFIG / common.NODES_DIR}/<name>/"
                            f" whose `scope` is {argv[1]} and `ssh` its alias, or this host as that node)")
                else:
                    node = f"; {argv[1]} is open, and an open scope's yard is never a node"
                print(f"scopes set: {yard} is not a host {common.CONFIG / 'lighthouse/hosts'} lists"
                      f" ({', '.join(hosts) or 'it lists none'}){node} — a yard is one of them, or none", file=sys.stderr)
                return 1
        why = None if "--dry-run" in rest else primary_main(REGISTRY.resolve())
        if why:
            print(f"✗ scopes set: {why} — write a claude/* worktree's copy: "
                  f"CLAUDE_REPOS_JSON=<that worktree>/repos.json scopes set …", file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        try:
            lines = set_flags(REGISTRY.resolve(), argv[1], want, login, "--no-login" in rest, "--dry-run" in rest, yard)
        except (ValueError, TypeError) as e:
            print(f"✗ scopes set: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        print("\n".join(lines))
        if "--dry-run" not in rest and lines[0].startswith("wrote"):
            print("  the registry is a file in git: commit it on a claude/* branch of its repo")
        return 0
    if cmd == "configure":
        if not sys.stdin.isatty() or "--dry-run" in argv[1:]:
            for name, scope in scopes.items():
                print(f"  {flag_line(name, scope)}")
            print("  (not a terminal: `scopes set SCOPE --confidential|--open [--login LABEL]` writes one)")
            return 0
        why = primary_main(REGISTRY.resolve())
        if why:
            print(f"✗ scopes configure: {why} — run it on a claude/* worktree's copy: "
                  f"CLAUDE_REPOS_JSON=<that worktree>/repos.json scopes configure", file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        print("\n".join(configure(REGISTRY.resolve())))
        return 0
    if cmd == "paths" and len(argv) == 2:
        if argv[1] not in scopes:
            print(f"scopes paths: no scope {argv[1]}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        print("\n".join(str(p) for p in common.scope_paths(argv[1])))
        return 0
    if cmd in ("of", "anchor", "workspace") and len(argv) == 2:
        key = resolve(argv[1], repos)
        name = scope_of(key, repos, scopes)
        if not name:
            print(f"scopes: {argv[1]} is in no scope", file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        if cmd == "of":
            print(name)
            return 0
        if cmd == "anchor":
            print(anchor_of(scopes[name], repos))
            return 0
        path = projects() / scopes[name]["file"]
        if not path.is_file():
            print(f"scopes: {name}'s file {path} does not exist — a session start writes it (any"
                  f" start for an open scope, one inside it for a confidential one), or {SELF} write", file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        print(path)
        return 0
    if cmd == "write":
        rest, src, stray, i = argv[1:], None, [], 0
        while i < len(rest):
            if rest[i] == "--dry-run":
                i += 1
            elif rest[i] == "--from" and src is None and i + 1 < len(rest) and rest[i + 1] \
                    and not rest[i + 1].startswith("-"):   # `--from ""` (an unset variable) is refused too
                src, i = rest[i + 1], i + 2
            else:
                stray, i = stray + [rest[i]], i + 1
        if stray:   # `--from=PATH`, a misspelt flag, `--from --dry-run`: refused, never read as a write of all
            print(f"scopes write [--dry-run] [--from PATH] — not understood: {' '.join(stray)}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        lock = common.CONFIG / "wharf" / "scopes-write.lock"
        lock.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        with open(lock, "w") as held:
            # sessions starting at once write one after another: each run's `only` differs (a
            # confidential session's scope is not an open one's), so a run never skips for another's
            # (the /finish review, 2026-11-03). A session start's run gives up after LOCK_WAIT with
            # exit 3 and no ✓, which sync.py reads as "nothing judged" and keeps its record; by hand, wait
            deadline = time.monotonic() + LOCK_WAIT
            while True:
                try:
                    fcntl.flock(held, fcntl.LOCK_EX | (fcntl.LOCK_NB if src else 0))
                    break
                except BlockingIOError:
                    if time.monotonic() >= deadline:
                        print(f"scopes write: another write held the lock for {LOCK_WAIT:g} s — not run",
                              file=sys.stderr)
                        return 3
                    time.sleep(0.5)
            # the lock first, then the registry: a run that waited judges and writes from what the
            # holder left, never a view read before it (the second finish review, 2026-11-03)
            data = registry_data()
            repos, scopes, names = registry(data=data)
            only, retired = None, data.get("scope_files_retired") or []
            if src:
                # the fence (knowledge-layers D3): a session outside a confidential scope never reads its
                # folders, so it never writes that scope's file either — a session inside it does. Open
                # means the flag is absent or false; any other value (`"true"`, 1) is fenced as
                # confidential, the safe side (the second finish review)
                only = {n for n, sc in scopes.items()
                        if sc.get("confidential") is None or sc.get("confidential") is False or inside(n, src, data)}
                retired = []   # moving one aside stays a deliberate run by hand

                def torn(sc):
                    ms = sc.get("repos") if isinstance(sc, dict) else None
                    return ms is not None and not (isinstance(ms, list) and all(isinstance(m, str) for m in ms))
                if common.registry_malformed(data) or any(torn(sc) for n, sc in scopes.items() if n not in only):
                    # a fenced scope whose repos do not read hides none of them: refuse in words that name
                    # nothing, as the core's fences read such a registry as torn (the second finish review)
                    print("✗ the registry has a malformed confidential scope — `" + SELF + " check` in a"
                          " session inside it names it", file=sys.stderr)
                    return 1
            bad = faults(repos, scopes)
            if bad and only is not None:
                # a fault naming a confidential scope this session is outside (its name, a repo of it) is
                # kept in this repo's record and said at its start: one line that names none of it instead
                # (the /finish review, 2026-11-03); `bin/scopes check` inside the scope names it
                hidden = set()
                for n, sc in scopes.items():
                    if n not in only:
                        try:
                            hidden |= {n, *members(sc, repos)}
                        except Exception:   # a malformed scope: its name alone, and nothing else is shown
                            hidden.add(n)
                word = re.compile(r"(?<![\w-])(" + "|".join(map(re.escape, sorted(hidden, key=len, reverse=True)))
                                  + r")(?![\w-])") if hidden else None
                kept = [b for b in bad if not (word and word.search(b))]
                if len(kept) < len(bad):
                    kept.append("the registry has a fault in a confidential scope this session is outside —"
                                f" `{SELF} check` in a session inside it names it")
                bad = kept
            if bad:
                print("\n".join(f"✗ {b}" for b in bad), file=sys.stderr)
                return 1
            lines = write(repos, scopes, names, projects(), "--dry-run" in rest, retired, only=only)
        print("\n".join(lines) if lines else f"✓ {len(only if only is not None else scopes)} scope files current")
        return 1 if any(l.startswith("✗") for l in lines) else 0
    if cmd == "check":
        bad = faults(repos, scopes)
        more, notes = confidential_faults(repos, scopes, projects())
        bad += more
        if bad:
            print("\n".join([f"✗ {b}" for b in bad] + [f"  {n}" for n in notes]))
            return 1
        conf = [n + (f" ({scopes[n]['login']})" if scopes[n].get("login") else "") for n in scopes
                if scopes[n].get("confidential") is True]
        print("\n".join([f"✓ scopes: {len(repos)} repos in {len(scopes)} scopes; confidential: {', '.join(conf) or 'none'}"]
                        + [f"  {n}" for n in notes]))
        return 0
    print(__doc__.split("\n\n")[0] + "\n" + __doc__.split("\n\n")[1], file=sys.stderr)
    return 2


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
